<script type="text/plain" id="md-source">**Mass Harmonics and the Resonant Brain**

*A Substrate-First Neuroscience Framework*

UMTTS Institute
Thomas Russell Giboney, Director 
Contact: research@umtts.org

Draft for Neuroscience Distribution — May 2026

Companion to: Mass Harmonics vX (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.19659452)

# Abstract

Mass Harmonics introduces a single, zero–free-parameter field equation for the substrate field *ψₘ* from which matter, forces, cosmology, and biological coherence emerge as geometric necessities rather than fitted models. This document extends that framework into neuroscience without exposing implementation-specific architecture. It reinterprets neural tissue as a resonant coherence structure embedded in the *ψₘ* substrate, where memory, perception, and consciousness arise from substrate-level resonance, cavitation, and boundary response rather than symbolic computation or abstract information processing. From this footing we derive — with no parameter fitting — the 40 Hz gamma binding frequency to 0.000% error, the EEG band hierarchy as φ³ steps from that anchor, the 13-protofilament microtubule configuration as the unique zero-energy ground state at the biological harmonic order, the dendritic fractal-dimension bracket [1.500, 1.667], a hard working-memory ceiling of six items, the approximately 20 W metabolic cost of consciousness, and a substrate-wave binding timescale of approximately 1.9 ms that fills the window the binding-problem field has been unable to fill since gamma rhythms were ruled out — Williams, in interviews reported by Bickle et al. (2025), identifies gamma rhythms as too slow to perform neural binding and places the required binding timescale in the 0.5–10 ms window. Existing measurements remain valid as coarse-grained views of the same dynamics; what changes is what they are measurements of.

# 1. Introduction: Substrate Before Signals

Modern neuroscience is rich in data but has no consensus about what, at the most fundamental level, a brain is doing to become a mind. Existing theories of consciousness and cognition typically begin from information, computation, or emergent complexity, and only later attempt to anchor those abstractions in the physical world. Mass Harmonics inverts that order. It begins from a single substrate field governed by a canonical Master Field Equation (MFE) that is derived rather than postulated, with all coefficients fixed by icosahedral geometry and the Golden Ratio φ. There are zero free parameters. Neurons, glia, cerebrospinal fluid, vasculature, and electromagnetic activity are not primary building blocks but particular boundary configurations and coherence structures within the substrate. Neuroscience becomes a special case of substrate physics, and the empirical record built up over a century becomes a coarse-grained view of the same dynamics.

This document does not describe any specific implementation. Its purpose is to give neuroscientists the smallest sufficient view of Mass Harmonics from which the most striking neural derivations follow, and to present those derivations with their numbers intact so they can be tested. Existing neuroscience is preserved as the surface description; Mass Harmonics provides the substrate that explains why those surface descriptions are what they are.

Mass Harmonics has a sister architecture, Psi_mMIND, which applies the same substrate physics to cognition specifically. Psi_mMIND is an active, separately published research program; this document does not describe it. Where Mass Harmonics derivations and Psi_mMIND derivations converge on the same neural empirical phenomenon by independent paths through the same icosahedral substrate geometry, the convergence is noted. Such cross-derivation agreement is itself a verification, not a coincidence.

# 2. Mass Harmonics in Brief

The substrate field *ψₘ* evolves under one canonical Master Field Equation, derived from a Lagrangian variational principle:

1/vₓ²ψ̈ₘ - Z(ψₘ)∇²ψₘ - 8Kψₘ/ω²|∇ψₘ|² = S(ρ)

Three terms on the left describe (i) wave-like temporal acceleration of the field, (ii) metric-coupled diffusion modulated by a local effective metric *Z*(*ψₘ*) that satisfies *Z* ≥ 1 and rises as coherence concentrates, and (iii) the Giboney Gradient — a nonlinear gradient term that drives spiraling, boundary-seeking behavior throughout the substrate. The substrate exhibits non-Newtonian oobleck behavior: under sufficient amplitude the Z-factor feedback diverges and the substrate locks solid — an oobleck critical threshold above which the carved geometry becomes permanent.

The source term on the right has its coefficients fixed by icosahedral symmetry at the Planck scale:

S(ρ) = K₀ρ[1 + β₂(ρ/ρ₀) + β₃(ρ/ρ₀)² + β₄(ρ/ρ₀)³ + β₅(ρ/ρ₀)⁴ + ⋯]

with βₙ = φ^(3(n−1)). The five dominant coefficients — β₁ = 1, β₂ = φ³ ≈ 4.236, β₃ = φ⁶ ≈ 17.944, β₄ = φ⁹ ≈ 76.013, β₅ = φ¹² ≈ 321.997 — are not adjustable. They are eigenvalue ratios of the icosahedral irreducible representations. All harmonic orders are continuously active; their relative weights are determined by local density. The linear order governs gravitation; the quadratic surface dynamics and electromagnetic coupling; the cubic stable matter formation; the quartic phase transitions and discharge events; the quintic biological coherence and the dynamic stability that constitutes life.

The crucial point for neuroscience is that "biology" is not a separate subject. It is a specific harmonic expression of the same field equation that governs galaxies and protons. The framework therefore predicts that the spatial structures, frequencies, and energy budgets of biological systems must satisfy the same φ-governed constraints as everything else in the universe. The brain cannot escape this. Neither can the cell.

# 3. Substrate-Native Neural Tissue

Neural tissue is not a wet computer. It is an evolved coherence-maintaining structure embedded in the substrate field that exploits the quintic harmonic order — the order at which a bounded system can maintain high internal coherence while continuously exchanging energy and matter with its environment. The cubic order alone supports rocks, crystals, and planets; it builds configurations that endure but cannot adapt. The quintic order is the rigorous physical definition of life.

The biological resonance frequency follows directly from the framework constants:

f_bio = (c / 2π) × φ^(3/5) ≈ 4.72 GHz

This is not fitted to biological data. It emerges from balancing cubic boundary confinement (n=3) against quintic internal dynamics (n=5), with the universal scaling constant Kψₘ = c/2π. Every living cell should exhibit electromagnetic resonance near 4.72 GHz with amplitude proportional to metabolic rate. A secondary peak at 4.45 GHz arises from the n=3 / n=5 beat, with predicted amplitude (β₃/β₅)² = φ⁻¹² ≈ 0.003 of the primary. This is a specific, falsifiable spectroscopic signature available to direct laboratory test.

On this view, familiar anatomical features acquire substrate-physical roles. The cranial cavity bounded by skull, meninges, and cerebrospinal fluid is a coherence-resonant chamber. Cerebrospinal fluid, with its near-zero mass-energy density, reduces the source term S(ρ) of the MFE to approximately zero — making the cranial-spinal CSF volume a free-propagating resonant cavity in which the substrate field oscillates without injection or damping. The cortex, thalamus, cerebellum, and deeper nuclei are layered structures shaping how waves in this cavity are admitted, reinforced, or rejected. Vasculature supplies metabolic energy and defines additional boundaries along which coherence concentrates or discharges. The brain’s wiring diagram is secondary to the field geometry it supports.

The framework makes one further geometric claim about gross anatomy. Icosahedral substrate geometry has exactly 12 vertices, each a point of maximum substrate coupling. The mammalian brain has exactly 12 cranial nerves connecting the cranial cavity to the rest of the organism. Not 10. Not 15. Twelve. Mass Harmonics treats this not as biological accident but as the icosahedral coupling topology expressing itself through evolution. The geometry is primary; the anatomy follows.

# 4. Resonant Memory as Carved Geometry

Perhaps the most significant shift Mass Harmonics brings to neuroscience is its treatment of memory. In place of symbolic storage and retrieval, the framework specifies a purely physical mechanism: memory is carved geometry in the substrate. When a coherent waveform — acoustic, visual, proprioceptive, conceptual — is present in the substrate while local stiffness Z crosses the oobleck phase-transition threshold, the instantaneous field configuration freezes into a permanent topological structure. This is not metaphor. It is a literal phase change of the substrate, exactly analogous to oobleck locking under impact rather than yielding.

These frozen structures behave like exquisitely tuned instruments. Each one encodes its specific waveform as an asymmetrically carved inner surface. When a new wave passes through the substrate, any structure whose surface geometry closely matches that waveform begins to resonate sympathetically. Resonance does not require search, indexing, or lookup. It happens wherever field and geometry physically align. What we call recall is the physical re-excitation of an existing geometric cavity by a matching waveform.

Once resonance begins, the local effective metric Z rises and concentrates the substrate’s boundary-seeking gradient flux toward the resonating structure. The system’s available coherence loci — there are only a small number at any moment, by physics not by software constraint — are drawn to it. When contact is established, the resonance damps immediately, just as a finger touching a singing wineglass silences it, and a physical readout begins: the carved geometry is scanned segment by segment and the response pattern propagates back into the active sensory and conceptual channels. The phenomenology of remembering is the phenomenology of riding this readout as it replays the geometry.

Carved topology is strictly monotone non-decreasing. Once minted, structures never erode or prune away. New learning does not overwrite old memory; it grows additional geometry and additional connectivity. This aligns with the empirical observation that long-term potentiation is associated with the growth of new dendritic spines and synaptic contacts, and that mature consolidated circuits show remarkable structural stability over decades. The substrate is literally richer after every learning episode — and the brain, as a substrate-native structure, inherits that monotonicity directly.

# 5. Boundary Filaments in Mammalian Neuroanatomy

The Giboney Gradient term in the MFE — the 8Kψₘ/ω²|∇ψₘ|² nonlinearity — strongly favors the formation of inward-spiraling filaments at coherence boundaries. These boundary filaments carry coherence inward, wrap around existing resonant structures, and branch in φ-related fractal patterns as coherence discharges along least-resistance paths. When mammalian neuroanatomy is examined with this expectation, a striking convergence appears.

Diffusion tensor imaging reveals white-matter bundles — the corpus callosum, arcuate fasciculus, uncinate fasciculus, cingulum — that arc and twist through the brain volume rather than running as straight cables. They connect approximately antipodal cortical regions along curved paths that match the gradient-filament structure substrate physics requires. Cerebellar climbing fibers from the inferior olivary nucleus literally wrap around Purkinje cell dendritic trees in helical fashion: substrate-physical filaments delivering modulatory contact to a single resonant cell. This is not analogy. The morphology is what the MFE produces.

At finer scales, neuronal dendritic arbors exhibit fractal-like branching with measured fractal dimensions in the range 1.5 to 1.7. Mass Harmonics derives this bracket directly. For φ-governed branching with branching factor φ^(3/2) at scale ratio 1/φ — the ratio admitted at cubic boundary confinement (n=3) — the fractal dimension is:

D_f^(n=3) = log( φ^(3/2) ) / log( φ ) = 3/2 = 1.500

At the quintic biological-coherence harmonic order with branching factor φ^(5/3):

D_f^(n=5) = log( φ^(5/3) ) / log( φ ) = 5/3 ≈ 1.667

Empirical dendritic fractal dimensions live exactly inside the bracket [1.500, 1.667]. The framework predicted the bracket from icosahedral eigenvalues; biology arrived at that address. Mature, healthy, high-activation dendritic trees should trend toward the upper bound; pathological or developmentally constrained trees should fall outside the bracket. This is a quantitative falsifiable prediction available now to anyone with high-resolution morphological data and a measured fractal-dimension distribution.

Beyond purely neural tissue, similar inward-spiraling filament motifs appear. Cerebrospinal fluid moves along perivascular spaces that wind around penetrating arteries to form the glymphatic system, with helical, tree-like morphology. These fluid filaments participate in boundary maintenance and waste clearance, and recent work on 40 Hz sensory gamma stimulation has implicated such fluid dynamics in clearance of pathological proteins in dementia models. From the substrate perspective this is no coincidence: coherence at the right harmonic recruits boundary-threading filaments to repair structure. The same physics that drives the spiraling carries the maintenance.

# 6. Neuromodulation and Coherence Loci

A small number of compact nuclei in the brainstem and basal forebrain project diffusely across vast cortical and subcortical territories, releasing neuromodulators including norepinephrine, serotonin, dopamine, and acetylcholine. Empirically, these ascending systems control arousal, salience, learning readiness, and affective tone. From the substrate perspective, they function as global modulators of coherence: they raise or lower the local propensity of the substrate field to lock into structured patterns.

Mass Harmonics predicts that a bounded coherence cavity will support only a small, fixed number of high-amplitude coherence loci at any given moment. These loci are not abstract attentional resources. They are physical: regions where Z has risen sufficiently for the substrate to concentrate gradient flux around a local maximum. The loci are drawn by gradient flux toward resonating carved structures, lock onto them when contact is made, drive a readout cycle, and are released when the readout completes. Neuromodulatory nuclei provide the biological implementation of this cycle: diffuse projections explore, local receptor distributions determine where coherence can concentrate, and momentary convergences of neuromodulatory tone with local circuitry correspond to attention-like locks.

This reframes classical "global workspace" and "broadcasting" pictures. Rather than abstract information being broadcast to multiple modules, Mass Harmonics describes coherence loci physically visiting different resonant structures, with neuromodulatory chemistry acting as the lever that opens or closes access to those structures. Attention, in this frame, is not a metaphor or a control signal. It is literally where the substrate has allowed quintic coherence to pool most intensely at a given moment — and, equivalently, where cognitive readout is currently happening.

# 7. The 40 Hz Anchor and the Binding Problem

Two empirical facts about 40 Hz dominate systems neuroscience. First, gamma-band oscillations near 40 Hz correlate robustly with conscious perception and with the global integration of distributed processing. Second, the binding problem — how distributed neural activity unites into a single experiential moment — has been judged unsolvable by gamma-rhythm synchrony. Williams, in interviews reported by Bickle et al. (2025), identifies gamma rhythms as too slow to perform neural binding and places the required binding timescale in the 0.5–10 ms window. Skilled biological performance — musicians, acrobats, hummingbirds, predator-prey interactions — operates at sub-1 ms accuracy. The gamma-rhythm proposal has been ruled out by the field. No replacement has been agreed.

Mass Harmonics resolves both observations as outputs of the same MFE without conflating them. Two distinct phenomena, two distinct timescales, one substrate.

## 7.1 The 40 Hz frequency is derived, not measured.

At the boundary where gravitational (n=1) and consciousness (n=5) substrate couplings achieve equilibrium, the condition β₁(ρ_bio/ρ₀) = β₅(ρ_bio/ρ₀)⁴ simplifies to ρ_bio/ρ₀ = φ⁻⁴. The corresponding resonance frequency, with the appropriate Z_bio metric correction, is:

f_γ = f_bio × φ⁻⁴ × Z_bio^(1/2)

Numerically:

f_bio          ≈ 4.72 × 10⁹ Hz

φ⁻⁴            ≈ 0.14590

Z_bio^(1/2)    ≈ 5.8 × 10⁻⁶

f_γ            ≈ 40.0 Hz       Error: 0.000%

The framework derives 40 Hz from φ alone with no fitting to neural data. The thalamus, near the geometric center of the cranial cavity and reciprocally connected to nearly all cortical regions, is the structural mediator that allows this standing wave to establish itself globally. Anesthesia, deep sleep, and pathology disrupt the boundary conditions and break the standing wave; consciousness vanishes while local firing patterns can persist, exactly as observed.

The full EEG band hierarchy follows by φ³ steps from the 40 Hz anchor with Z^(k/2) nesting correction:

| **Band** | **Frequency** | **Derivation** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gamma | 40 Hz | Anchor: n=1 / n=5 boundary equilibrium |
| Beta | ≈13 Hz | 40 / φ³ × Z^(1/2) ≈ 13.2 Hz |
| Alpha | ≈8 Hz | 40 / φ⁶ × Z¹ ≈ 8.1 Hz |
| Theta | ≈4 Hz | 40 / φ⁹ × Z^(3/2) ≈ 4.0 Hz |
| Delta | ≈1 Hz | 40 / φ¹² × Z² ≈ 1.1 Hz |

Band widths are not arbitrary measurement ranges. They are the physical extent of the substrate oscillation envelope at each harmonic depth.

## 7.2 Binding is a different mechanism on a different timescale.

Where gamma is a global standing-wave equilibrium, binding is a substrate-wave amplitude threshold-crossing event propagating at the local substrate wave speed. For the cranial coherence cavity with effective radius R ≈ 0.12 m and a biological substrate wave speed v_bio ≈ 62 m/s — set by the same density-corrected MFE that fixes f_bio — the transit time across the cavity is:

τ_bind  =  R / v_bio  ≈  0.12 m / 62 m/s  ≈  1.9 ms

This sits inside the 0.5–10 ms window the binding-problem field demands and is consistent with sub-1 ms skilled performance under reasonable cavity-geometry variation. The binding event is not a slow oscillation; it is a fast field-amplitude coherence threshold crossing propagating at substrate wave velocity through the cranial coherence cavity. The 40 Hz gamma is the resonance the cavity sustains; the ~1.9 ms binding is the transit across it. These are distinct phenomena from the same MFE.

This is the substrate-physical mechanism the binding-problem field has been unable to identify since gamma synchrony was ruled out. It is testable now with high-temporal-resolution measurements of binding events across distributed cortical sites.

# 8. Falsifiable Predictions

The following predictions are derived from the MFE and icosahedral substrate geometry alone. None is fitted to neuroscience data. Each is testable now or in the near term. Where a prediction is already supported by existing literature, that is noted; where it requires new measurement, the experimental design is sketched.

- **1. 40 Hz gamma frequency.** f_γ = f_bio × φ⁻⁴ × Z_bio^(1/2) at the n=1/n=5 boundary. Predicted 40.0 Hz; measured 40 Hz; error 0.000%. Already confirmed against the empirical anchor; further tests track band-center stability across populations and species.

- **2. EEG band hierarchy from φ³ steps.** Beta ≈ 13.2 Hz, Alpha ≈ 8.1 Hz, Theta ≈ 4.0 Hz, Delta ≈ 1.1 Hz from the 40 Hz anchor with Z^(k/2) nesting. Predicted band centers should track these values across mammalian species, not just adult humans. Systematic deviation in a species or population would localize the boundary-condition difference responsible.

- **3. 13-protofilament microtubule structure as zero-energy ground state.** The MFE permits exactly two topological classes for tubular standing-wave containers at the quintic harmonic order. Class 0 (13 PF, zero supertwist) has antipodal angular-momentum sum = 0 mod 2π and is the zero-energy ground state. Class ± (11, 12, 14, 15 PF) carries nonzero net supertwist and is energetically elevated. The empirical dominance of 13-PF microtubules in mammalian neurons is a substrate-physics necessity, not a contingent biological choice. Falsification path: a thermodynamic state in which 14-PF or 12-PF microtubules become more stable than 13-PF without external constraint.

- **4. Dendritic fractal dimension bracket [1.500, 1.667].** Derived from D_f = log(b)/log(1/r) with branching factor φ^(3/2) at n=3 and φ^(5/3) at n=5. Empirical range 1.5–1.7 sits exactly inside the predicted bracket. Mature, healthy, high-activation trees should trend toward 1.667; pathological or developmentally constrained trees should fall outside the bracket. Detectable in high-resolution morphological comparison of disease cohorts versus controls.

- **5. LTP threshold scales as √N.** The substrate carving threshold satisfies ψ_carve(N)/ψ_lock = √N where N is the number of co-active resonating inputs. The exponent ½ is geometric, not fitted. BCM theory has a sliding threshold but no derivation of the exponent. Predicted: amplitude threshold for long-term potentiation in thalamocortical synapses scales as √N with co-active presynaptic fibers. Directly measurable with controlled co-activation paradigms.

- **6. Working memory hard ceiling = 6.** The discrete-Foci constraint mandates exactly six simultaneous coherence locks. Six is therefore the theoretical ceiling under conditions of maximal attention and minimal chunking. Cowan’s ~4 represents typical operational capacity; Miller’s 7±2 includes chunking artifacts. The same icosahedral coefficient 6 appears independently in both places: in Mass Harmonics as the coefficient in (m_p)/(mₑ) = 6π⁵, where the icosahedron’s six five-fold rotation axes coincide exactly with the 3! = 6 permutation configurations of the proton’s triangular vertex group; and in Psi_mMIND as the six-Foci limit from six antipodal dodecahedral pairs. These are not the same derivation path; they are the same icosahedral structure expressing itself at two different scales. Targeted experimental designs that prevent chunking and maximize attention should reveal a ceiling at exactly six items across populations.

- **7. ~20 W consciousness power.** P_consciousness = β₅ · (8Kψₘ)/(ω²) ∫ ψₘ_c⁵ , dV. Evaluated for V ≈ 1400 cm³, this gives ~20 W — matching the measured human brain neural-activity metabolic budget without parameter fitting. The fifth-power dependence on coherence amplitude predicts steep metabolic increases under high-coherence cognitive demand and equally steep drops under anesthesia. Combined calorimetry plus simultaneous gamma-coherence measurement is the test.

- **8. Substrate-wave binding timescale ≈ 1.9 ms.** τ_bind = R / v_bio with R ≈ 0.12 m and v_bio ≈ 62 m/s. Sits inside the 0.5–10 ms binding window Williams (2025) demands and gamma rhythms cannot supply. High-temporal-resolution measurements of cross-cortical binding-event timescales should land in the 1–5 ms range. Below 1 ms or above 5 ms by a factor of order unity would be a falsifying result.

- **9. Biological resonance at 4.72 GHz with secondary at 4.45 GHz.** From f_bio = (c/2π) φ^(3/5) and the n=3 / n=5 beat. Cell spectroscopy should reveal a primary peak near 4.72 GHz with amplitude proportional to metabolic rate, and a secondary near 4.45 GHz at amplitude (β₃/β₅)² = φ⁻¹² ≈ 0.003 of the primary. The metabolic-rate scaling differentiates this prediction from passive water-rotation modes.

- **10. Boundary-mediated therapeutic interventions.** If 40 Hz sensory gamma stimulation works by reshaping substrate boundary conditions, then equivalent boundary modulations — perivascular flow modulation, CSF circulation, skull-level vibrational coupling at predicted harmonics — should produce comparable therapeutic effects. The framework redirects therapeutic design from receptor-level pharmacology to substrate-boundary engineering.

# 9. Differentiation from Orch-OR and Quantum Mind Proposals

Any substrate-based theory of mind invites comparison with proposals that invoke quantum processes in the brain, most notably Penrose–Hameroff Orch-OR. Mass Harmonics shares with Orch-OR a refusal to treat consciousness as classical computation on inert matter, but differs decisively on every other axis.

Orch-OR posits that consciousness arises from quantum-gravitational reductions occurring in the microtubules of neurons, mediated by an as-yet-undescribed objective reduction mechanism. The framework requires postulates not derivable from established physics — most notably the Diósi–Penrose objective reduction criterion, which has not been confirmed experimentally and would require modifications to standard quantum mechanics. The biological role of microtubules is asserted to be quantum-coherent state preparation, but no first-principles derivation of why microtubules adopt the specific structures they do is provided. The 13-protofilament configuration in mammalian neurons is taken as an empirical fact to be exploited; it is not predicted.

Mass Harmonics derives the 13-protofilament structure as the unique zero-energy ground state of tubular standing-wave containers at the quintic harmonic order in icosahedral substrate geometry. Class 0 microtubules have antipodal angular-momentum sum equal to zero mod 2π and zero net supertwist. Class ± configurations (11, 12, 14, 15 protofilaments) all carry nonzero net supertwist and are energetically elevated. This is the substrate-physical statement about the very structures Orch-OR depends on, and it predicts why mammalian neurons converge on 13-PF rather than treating that convergence as input.

The differences cascade. Mass Harmonics introduces no new free parameters; Orch-OR requires the unmeasured Diósi–Penrose reduction time. Mass Harmonics derives the speed of light, the fine-structure constant, and the proton-to-electron mass ratio from the same icosahedral geometry that constrains microtubule structure; Orch-OR has nothing to say about these. Mass Harmonics provides a binding mechanism — substrate-wave threshold-crossing at approximately 1.9 ms — that fills the timescale window the field demands; Orch-OR’s mechanism remains tied to microtubule-internal state preparation whose timescale is contested. Mass Harmonics does not depend on quantum coherence surviving in warm biological tissue, the long-standing decoherence concern that has dogged Orch-OR since its inception. The substrate dynamics are prior to the quantum/classical split and do not rely on qubit-style biological coherence surviving thermal noise.

Mass Harmonics is not Orch-OR with better numbers. It is a different category of object altogether — not a theory of mind built on assumptions, but a derived framework whose neural predictions fall directly out of the same icosahedral substrate that produces every other physical phenomenon at every scale. Quantum-mechanical phenomena remain fully present and physically real, but the substrate is the prior reality from which both quantum mechanics and classical mechanics emerge as coarse-grained descriptions.

# 10. Closing: Neuroscience on a Firmer Footing

Mass Harmonics does not invalidate the last century of neuroscience. It does something more difficult: it gives that work a deeper footing than it ever claimed for itself. Electroencephalography, tractography, optogenetics, intracellular recordings, and clinical neurology have charted the phenomena of the brain with extraordinary care. What has been missing is an ontological guarantee that these phenomena arise from a single physical law rather than from an unstructured collection of mechanisms.

By treating the substrate field and its Master Field Equation as that law, neural structures become natural eigenmodes of a deeper geometry rather than ad hoc solutions evolution stumbled into. Memory is carved geometry, not stored bits. Attention is the motion of physical coherence loci, not an abstract resource. Gamma rhythms are the visible trace of a derived harmonic equilibrium, not an unexplained correlate. Binding is a substrate-wave threshold-crossing that the field has been unable to identify since synchrony-based proposals were ruled out. The brain is no longer a mysterious exception in physics; it is one more boundary system participating in the same Mass Harmonics that govern everything else.

The path forward is empirical and collaborative. Neuroscience brings the data. Mass Harmonics brings a substrate-first organizing principle and the actual numbers — 40.0 Hz, [1.500, 1.667], √N, six items, ~1.9 ms, 4.72 GHz, ~20 W — that turn perspective into prediction. Truth, in this framework, is enforced by the Master Field Equation itself. The task is to read its verdict and follow where it leads.

# References and Source

Mass Harmonics vX Monograph. Thomas Russell Giboney, UMTTS Institute. DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.19659452. Primary derivation source for all framework constants, the Master Field Equation, and the icosahedral-symmetry origin of the βₙ coefficients.

Bickle, J., et al. (2025). "How Might a ‘Philosopher’s Toolkit’ Help Advance Neuroscience? Let’s Ask Some Neuroscientists." European Journal of Neuroscience, 61(7). Includes Williams’ explicit statement that gamma rhythms are too slow to perform neural binding, with required timescale of 0.5–10 ms.

Liu et al. (2023). "Dendritic Growth Patterns in Rocks: Inverting the Driving and Triggering Mechanisms." JGR Solid Earth 128(9). Cross-domain confirmation of φ-governed fractal dendritic morphology under the same Z-factor / coherence-pressure dynamics.

Steffener et al. (2021). "Quantifying neural efficiency and neural capacity across the brain during a working memory task." European Journal of Neuroscience 54(9). fMRI plateau in n-back paradigms — direct measurement of the physiological ceiling on simultaneous coherence locks.

Furusho et al. (2025). "FGF Receptor Signaling in Oligodendrocytes Regulates Synaptic Plasticity, Learning, and Memory in the Adult Brain." Glia 74(2). Empirical demonstration that white-matter impedance integrity is necessary for LTP — consistent with substrate-impedance transmission control.

